/*!
 * \page timertt_getting_started Getting Started

\section timertt_getting_started__where_to_get Where To Get The Library

There is several methods for obtaining timertt library.

1. The single header file with all library code can be download as archive from
<a
href="https://sourceforge.net/projects/sobjectizer/files/timertt/">SourceForge
site</a>. Those archives have names like timertt-x.y.x-headeronly.7z. For
example: timertt-1.2.0-headeronly.7z.

2. The archive with library, samples, tests and documentation can be downloaded as archive from 
<a
href="https://sourceforge.net/projects/sobjectizer/files/timertt/">SourceForge
site</a>. Those archives have names like timertt-x.y.x-full.7z. For
example: timertt-1.2.0-full.7z.

3. The source code of library can be exported or checked out from <a
href="https://sourceforge.net/projects/sobjectizer/">SObjectizer</a>
Subversion repository. Releases are inside this <a
href="http://svn.code.sf.net/p/sobjectizer/repo/tags/timertt">tree</a>,
development versions are inside this <a
href="http://svn.code.sf.net/p/sobjectizer/repo/branches/timertt">tree</a>.

\section timertt_getting_started__add_to_the_project Adding To The Project

timertt is a header-only library. All the code in single header file
<tt>timertt/all.hpp</tt>. So to add timertt to your project is only necessary
to add path to <tt>timertt/all.hpp</tt> to INCLUDE path.

\section timertt_getting_started__basic_usage_of_timer_threads Basic Usage of Timer Threads

An object of appropriate thread type must be created. For example:
\code
using timer_wheel_thread = timertt::timer_wheel_thread_template<
		timertt::default_timer_action_type,
		timertt::default_error_logger,
		timertt::default_actor_exception_handler >;
timer_wheel_thread tt;
\endcode

Then timer thread must be started by calling start() method:
\code
timer_wheel_thread tt;
tt.start();
\endcode

\attention Timers must be added to timer thread only after the start of timer thread.

There are two kind of timers: single-shot and periodic. Single-shot timers
are created by activate() methods with only \a pause argument. Periodic timers
are created by activate() methods with \a pause and \a period arguments:

\code
using timer_wheel_thread = timertt::timer_wheel_thread_template<
		timertt::default_timer_action_type,
		timertt::default_error_logger,
		timertt::default_actor_exception_handler >;
timer_wheel_thread tt;
tt.start();

// Adding single-shot timer.
tt.activate( std::chrono::milliseconds( 500 ),
  []() { std::cout << "Hello, World" << std::endl; } );

// Adding periodic timer.
tt.activate(
	// Pause before the first timer activation.
	std::chrono::milliseconds( 250 ),
	// Period of timer activations.
	std::chrono::milliseconds( 500 ),
	[]() { std::cout << "Hello, World. Again" << std::endl; } );
\endcode

Timers can be deactivated. To do so it is necessary to preallocate timer
and store timer identifier as timer_holder object. This object
then passed to activate() and deactivate() methods:
\code
using timer_wheel_thread = timertt::timer_wheel_thread_template<
		timertt::default_timer_action_type,
		timertt::default_error_logger,
		timertt::default_actor_exception_handler >;
timer_wheel_thread tt;
tt.start();

// Adding single-shot timer.
timer_wheel_thread::timer_holder id1 = tt.allocate();
tt.activate( id1, std::chrono::milliseconds( 500 ),
  []() { std::cout << "Hello, World" << std::endl; } );

// Adding periodic timer.
auto id2 == tt.allocate();
tt.activate(
	// Timer ID.
	id2,
	// Pause before the first timer activation.
	std::chrono::milliseconds( 250 ),
	// Period of timer activations.
	std::chrono::milliseconds( 500 ),
	[id2, &tt]() {
		static int invocations = 1;
		std::cout << "Hello, World. Again ("
				<< invocations << ")" << std::endl;
		if( ++invocations > 3 )
			tt.deactivate( id2 );
	} );
...
// Deactivate single-shot timer.
tt.deactivate( id1 );
\endcode

Timer thread must be stopped. It can be done several ways:

1. Timer thread is stopped automatically in timer object destructor.

2. There is a method shutdown_and_join() which sends shutdown signal
to the timer thread and waits the finishing of the timer thread:
\code
using timer_wheel_thread = timertt::timer_wheel_thread_template<
		timertt::default_timer_action_type,
		timertt::default_error_logger,
		timertt::default_actor_exception_handler >;
timer_wheel_thread tt;
tt.start();
...// Some work with timers...
tt.shutdown_and_join();
\endcode

3. There is a method shutdown() which only sends shutdown signal to
the thread, but no waits. And a method join() which waits the timer
thread. This methods can be useful if shutdown signal must be sent
from timer action:
\code
using timer_wheel_thread = timertt::timer_wheel_thread_template<
		timertt::default_timer_action_type,
		timertt::default_error_logger,
		timertt::default_actor_exception_handler >;
timer_wheel_thread tt;
tt.start();

tt.activate( std::chrono::milliseconds( 500 ),
		std::chrono::milliseconds( 1000 ),
		[&tt]()
		{
			if( some-condition )
				tt.shutdown();
			...
		} );

tt.join();
\endcode

\section timertt_getting_started__basic_usage_of_timer_managers Basic Usage of Timer Managers

An object of appropriate manager type must be created. For example:
\code
timertt::timer_wheel_manager_template< timertt::thread_safety::unsafe > tm;
\endcode

or:
\code
timertt::timer_heap_manager_template< timertt::thread_safety::safe > tm;
\endcode

Each timer manager parametrized by several template parameters. The first of
them is the most important: it defines thread safety for the manager. This
parameter can have one of the two values:

- timertt::thread_safety::unsafe. This value means that manager will not be
  thread safe. Only one thread can work with such manager. The behavior and
  consequences of using not-thread-safe manager from several parallel threads
  are undefined;
- timertt::thread_safety::safe. This value means that manager can be used from
  several parallel threads. But with some care: any thread can call
  allocate/activate/deactivate methods. But process_expired_timers and
  nearest_time_point/timeout_before_nearest_timer must be called on the context
  on just one thread.

The not-thread-safe manages (e.g. managers with thread_safety::unsafe
parameter) are intended for single-threading applications where just one
thread is used for all application tasks. Those managers do not use any
synchronization primitives (like mutexes or atomics).

The thread-safe manages (e.g. managers with thread_safety::safe parameter) are
intended for multi-threading applications where several threads can create and
schedule timers but just one thread handles them all. And this timer-handling
thread does some other work (perform event-loop actions for example). If
timer-handling thread does only timer-processing actions it is better to think
about using timer_threads (see description above).

Like with timer threads two kinds of timers are supported: single-shot and
periodic:
\code
timertt::timer_heap_manager_template< timertt::thread_safety::unsafe > tm( ... );
// Anonymous single-shot timer.
tm.activate( std::chrono::milliseconds(100), [] { ... } );
// Anonymous periodic timer.
tm.activate(
  std::chrono::milliseconds(100),
  std::chrono::milliseconds(500),
  [] { ... } );

// Preallocated single-shot timer.
auto id1 = tm.allocate();
tm.activate( id1, std::chrono::milliseconds(100), [] { ... } );
// Preallocated periodic timer.
auto id2 = tm.allocate();
tm.activate( id2,
  std::chrono::milliseconds(100),
  std::chrono::milliseconds(500),
  [] { ... } );

// Deactivation of timer.
auto id3 = tm.allocate();
tm.activate( id3,
  std::chrono::milliseconds(100),
  std::chrono::milliseconds(500),
  [id3, &tm] {
    ...
    tm.deactivate( id3 );
  } );
\endcode

Timer manages, unlike timer threads, have no its own working context for loop
of sleep-exec actions. Because of that the user of a timer manager must write
this loop by itself. Typically this loop will looks like:
\code
timertt::timer_heap_manager_template< timertt::thread_safety::unsafe > tm( ... );
...
while( true )
{
  process_some_application_specific_events();

  // Handle all expired timers.
  tm.process_expired_timers();
  // Sleep for the next timer expiration.
  std::this_thread::sleep_for(
    tm.timeout_before_nearest_timer( std::chrono::milliseconds(100) ) );
}
\endcode

There is another timer_manager method for handling timeouts before next timer
expiration: nearest_time_point, but it requires some attention:
\code
while( true )
{
  process_some_application_specific_events();

  // Handle all expired timers.
  tm.process_expired_timers();
  auto n = tm.nearest_time_point();
  if( std::get<0>( n ) )
  {
    // There is at least one timer.
    std::this_thread::sleep_until( std::get<1>( n ) );
  }
  else
    // There is no timers at all.
    // Do some other actions or simple finish work.
    wait_for_something();
}
\endcode

Please note that there is no need to start or stop timer manager: it is ready
for work just after creation. This is a big difference from timer threads: a
timer can be scheduled to a timer thread only after invocation of start method
for the timer thread. But allocate/activate methods for timer managers can be
called at any time.

\section timertt_getting_started__custom_timer_actions User defined Timer_Action types

Since v.1.2.0 there is a possibility to specify a custom type for Timer_Action.
For example:
\code
// This functor must be run when a timer expires.
class delayed_action_performer
{
	... // Some stuff.
public:
	... // Some public stuff.

	// This operator will be called by timertt.
	void operator()() const
	{
		... // Code to be run when a timer expires.
	}
};

timertt::timer_heap_manager_template<
		timertt::thread_safety::unsafe,
		// Specify type of user's Timer_Action.
		delayed_action_performer >
	tm( ... );

// Anonymous single-shot timer.
tm.activate( std::chrono::milliseconds(100), delayed_action_performer{...} );
// Anonymous periodic timer.
tm.activate(
  std::chrono::milliseconds(100),
  std::chrono::milliseconds(500),
  delayed_action_performer{...} );
\endcode

User-defined Timer_Action type must be Moveable and MoveConstructibe type.

 */

// vim:ft=cpp

